{"findings":[{"file":"cookiecutter/find.py","line":22,"what_goes_wrong":"When a repository contains multiple templated project directories, the function silently selects the first filesystem entry instead of rejecting the ambiguous template.","trigger":"A repository directory contains both `{{cookiecutter.one}}/` and `{{cookiecutter.two}}/`.","expected":"Raise `UnknownTemplateDirException`, as documented by that exception; actual: returns the first directory yielded by `os.listdir()`.","actual":"Returns one arbitrary candidate and generation proceeds from that template.","confidence":"high","verified_by_running":true,"reproduction":"from unittest.mock import patch\nfrom cookiecutter.exceptions import UnknownTemplateDirException\nfrom cookiecutter.find import find_template\n\nwith patch(\n    \"cookiecutter.find.os.listdir\",\n    return_value=[\"{{cookiecutter.one}}\", \"{{cookiecutter.two}}\"],\n):\n    try:\n        find_template(\"repository\")\n    except UnknownTemplateDirException:\n        pass\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(\"ambiguous templates were silently accepted\")"},{"file":"cookiecutter/config.py","line":106,"what_goes_wrong":"The returned default configuration shares its nested dictionaries with the module-level defaults, so mutating one caller's config changes later callers' defaults.","trigger":"Call `get_user_config(default_config=True)`, add an abbreviation to its `abbreviations` dictionary, then call it again.","expected":"Each returned configuration should be independent; actual: the added abbreviation appears in the second caller's configuration.","actual":"A shallow copy leaks mutations through `DEFAULT_CONFIG`.","confidence":"high","verified_by_running":true,"reproduction":"from cookiecutter.config import get_user_config\n\nfirst = get_user_config(default_config=True)\nfirst[\"abbreviations\"][\"temporary-test-abbreviation\"] = \"https://example.invalid/{}\"\nsecond = get_user_config(default_config=True)\nassert \"temporary-test-abbreviation\" not in second[\"abbreviations\"]"},{"file":"cookiecutter/config.py","line":112,"what_goes_wrong":"Whether an explicitly supplied default config path is loaded depends on Python object identity rather than its path value.","trigger":"Pass the exact `USER_CONFIG_PATH` string object as `config_file` when that file does not exist.","expected":"An explicit missing `config_file` should raise `ConfigDoesNotExistException`; actual: it is treated as though no config file was supplied and defaults are returned.","actual":"The `is not` comparison skips `get_config()` for the identical string object.","confidence":"high","verified_by_running":true,"reproduction":"from unittest.mock import patch\nfrom cookiecutter.config import get_user_config\nfrom cookiecutter.exceptions import ConfigDoesNotExistException\n\npath = \"missing-cookiecutterrc\"\nwith patch(\"cookiecutter.config.USER_CONFIG_PATH\", path), patch(\n    \"cookiecutter.config.os.path.exists\", return_value=False\n), patch.dict(\"os.environ\", {}, clear=True):\n    try:\n        get_user_config(path)\n    except ConfigDoesNotExistException:\n        pass\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(\"explicit missing config path was silently ignored\")"}],"what_examined":"Reviewed all production modules, focused on configuration, repository/template discovery, rendering, hooks, archives, and VCS paths; ran direct reproductions for the findings. I also invoked the suite and Ruff: the suite was unable to create its pytest temp directory because of sandbox permissions, so its resulting setup errors were not treated as code defects."}
